OPS-43(d): give a long solve something to watch

A direct solve is silent for its whole factorization. TH-11 step 5d went over
30 minutes without writing a line while entirely healthy, and the only
evidence it was alive was memory climbing, read by hand. Three levels,
stacking, all opt-in and default-off:

d1. FEM_EM_SOLVER_PROGRESS raises MUMPS ICNTL(4) so the solver reports its
    analysis and factorization phases and their memory estimates on rank 0.
    Unset -- every existing run and CI -- leaves the options dict
    byte-identical, so no recorded number can move, and an explicit
    solver_petsc_options still wins because it is applied afterwards.

d2. The same gate prints dof count, degree and cell count immediately before
    the call that dominates the window, and its elapsed time after. Rank 0
    only; never touches the solve.

d3. scripts/testing/watch_run.sh samples cgroup memory, rank count and summed
    CPU into a TSV from outside the run, so it reports when the process says
    nothing and survives the run's own death -- which is exactly what today's
    killed wrapper left me without. The memory column is the progress bar a
    direct solve does not have: it climbs through the factorization and
    plateaus when it completes.

Verified live against the running step 5d: 262.1 GiB flat at 672% CPU across
8 ranks, i.e. mid-factorization. Two traps are recorded in the script itself:
memory.peak is per container lifetime, and wrapping the watcher in timeout
kills its process group and reads as an unreachable container.

The d1/d2 gate -- one smoke solve with the variable set and one without,
identical results, extra lines only in the first -- is still owed and the row
says so.

